r to create plastics so as you  can see we've taken a longer  less useful hydrocarbon chain using heat  and a catalyst that we'll look at in a  minute  we've broken that down into a shorter  alkane which could be more useful  and an alkene which could be used for  lots of different things for example in  the process of polymerization  so that's the basics of cracking now  in a lab we might have an experiment  that looks a bit like this all this is  is a tube a glass tube  and in both ends there's a bung now what  we actually have  in there is we have  a catalyst now all the catalyst does is  speeds up the rate of reaction  without being used up a suitable  catalyst  is aluminium oxide  so if i was doing this in a lab what i  could do  is first of all i would heat because  remember it needs to be thermal  decomposition  so we need an element of heat i'd heat  up this  longer hydrocarbon  and vaporize it now once it was  vaporized  it could be passed through  this tube  while as it goes over the catalyst it  catalyzes the reaction  and therefore it's going to be broken  down into  our two products so coming out of the  other side  in the lab this is where we'd have a  shorter  alkane alkane  and also we'd have coming out uh alkene  so this is just an equipment that we  could use in the laboratory  in order to do a cracking experiment so  breaking that down  now the final thing we need to know  about cracking  is this here how it operates in industry  so when we have the fractional  distillation of crude oil  there's another video where you can  learn more about that but when we  basically a fraction distillation crude  oil  we have lots of different fractions  produced  these are fractions in order of how long  the  chain length is so lpg here  has three carbons in the chain so it's  very very short  whereas fuel oil and bitumen have an  average number of carbon atoms of around  80. so as we can see there's different  lengths  of within each fraction  what we can also see is if i was to  uh do the fractional distillation of  crude oil only  two percent of that um distillation  would be comprised of lpg whereas a  massive  50 percent of that would be fuel oil and  bitumen  now this is when it gets interesting  because we could see why cracking could  be so important  if we look at how much there is in oil  crude oil compared to how much is in  demand  we can see that these shorter chained  hydrocarbons particularly  lpg and petrol  we need them more than the  more than the amount that we produce  whereas  if i look at uh fuel oil and bitumen 50  of the constitution of crude oil is made  up from these fractions  but i only require about 38  so this is why we could do cracking in  order to break down  these longer less useful hydrocarbons  into these smaller more useful  hydrocarbons  and that basically is how cracking works  thank you
 

